# TypeScript strict

Follow the Cursor Directory TypeScript standard: strong types, no `any`, named interfaces, and explicit error handling. UI copy stays Polish. Type names stay English.

## No `any`

- `any`, `as any`, `Object`, and `@ts-ignore` are forbidden. `@ts-expect-error` needs a one-line reason.
- Use `unknown`, then narrow. A type assertion is allowed only after Zod `parse` / `safeParse` or a discriminated-union check.
- Prefer `import type` for type-only imports. Mark fields that callers must not reassign as `readonly`.
- Do not widen `IssuePriority`, `PropertyIssueStatus`, `IssueCategory`, `IssueSource`, or `ReporterType` to `string`.

## DOMIO data interfaces

Users, buildings, failures, and finances use named interfaces. Do not type these payloads as `Record<string, unknown>` or an inline object at the call site.

When `src/types/database.ts` (or this repo's equivalent) already exports the type, import it:

| Module | Types |
| --- | --- |
| Users | `OrgStaffMember`, `OrgMemberRow` |
| Buildings | `ManagerBuilding`, `BuildingContact` |
| Failures | `PropertyIssueInsert`, `PropertyIssueWithLocation` |
| Finances | `PropertyIssueBilling`, `PropertyIssueFinancialOriginal`, `OrgSerwisBillingSettings` |

New external input (forms, JSONB, API bodies) gets a Zod schema, then `z.infer<typeof schema>`. Money fields stay `number | null` in PLN, matching the billing interfaces. Validate them with `z.number().finite()` at the boundary.

## Errors

- `catch (error: unknown)`. Narrow with `instanceof Error` or `ZodError`. Do not use an empty `catch`.
- Log the operation and the error, then rethrow or return a typed failure. Do not continue on invalid data.
- Supabase `{ data, error }`: when `error` is set, stop. Do not read `data`.

```ts
const parsed = propertyIssueInsertSchema.safeParse(input);
if (!parsed.success) {
  console.error("Invalid property issue", { issues: parsed.error.issues });
  throw new TypeError("Invalid property issue");
}

const issue: PropertyIssueInsert = parsed.data;
```
